#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16dac5bd-acbd-51c6-8636-172e3a55aaf1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02397ef0-344b-58b8-90bb-d83ca89137c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}{csc}^{2}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266517} {:answer/id #uuid "687e5152-64e1-5f3c-9b5c-5fa8024ca85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266518} {:answer/id #uuid "73a8eb8c-3607-56b4-9de7-d3f5b5292467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266519} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e0cad9-9d17-511c-9372-47170f23edaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{csc}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266520} {:answer/id #uuid "a90f1fc1-65bc-5b75-a002-a1c8cf7666ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a90f1fc1-65bc-5b75-a002-a1c8cf7666ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(4t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266521} :answer-field/id #uuid "1788ef18-778e-5443-b559-964de5ac34c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266516}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0600f53b-9fb9-50fc-9a9b-d28a258d380f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122888" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-10,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+15.$ Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{24\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)}=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(3\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4t))}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12{csc}^{2}⁡(4t)}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16dac5bd-acbd-51c6-8636-172e3a55aaf1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "776acaeb-f992-5cea-bb78-00b08fd8b407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}{e}^{2-8t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266524} {:answer/id #uuid "4e925519-1db2-5bb5-a9c6-c508b906eb03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{2}{e}^{8-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266525} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa8c344-c348-5b10-a387-2139def3b16c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15{e}^{8t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266526} {:answer/id #uuid "c06ad589-eedd-5f6d-9209-413fe28f0204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{15}{e}^{-2-8t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266527} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6c8872-d724-52b7-b08b-183e87133ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2+4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c06ad589-eedd-5f6d-9209-413fe28f0204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{15}{e}^{-2-8t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266527} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a7bf77d-b817-5bde-beb0-e863897247b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266523}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "601fce0f-444c-5aa9-8cae-681efbedd5e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-122903" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=3{e}^{5t+1},\\,y=5{e}^{2t}-7$ where $t$ is a real number. Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=15{e}^{1+5t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=10{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{10{e}^{2t}}{15{e}^{1+5t}}=\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-1-3t}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-1-3t})}{15{e}^{1+5t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2{e}^{-1-3t}}{15{e}^{1+5t}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{15}{e}^{-2-8t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fb50541-88df-5f3f-b9e0-cf51edae401e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ccaa9ac-6693-54a9-8b45-1e346d481a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266531} {:answer/id #uuid "858cb3db-5191-5d55-a47d-21207204175d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "858cb3db-5191-5d55-a47d-21207204175d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266532} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a358d85-f4d7-515d-9468-6665228a47f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266530}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be541b4a-bb13-5d68-b1b5-d082754d05e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-348295" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x={t}^{2}+1,\\,y={t}^{3}+4.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}t,$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3}{2}t,$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2}+1)=2t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{3}{2}t)}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,2}=\\frac{3}{4(2)}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fb50541-88df-5f3f-b9e0-cf51edae401e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Parametric Curve at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f11ff6b-5cc9-55ab-a138-b73419ae3487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f11ff6b-5cc9-55ab-a138-b73419ae3487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266535} :answer-field/id #uuid "94b4cd57-ce0f-52f0-a0f2-0292fcc15d3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266534}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84d2b5b2-de55-52a6-af4a-f2e1349bfd06" :question/math-academy-id "q-352390" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x={t}^{5}+1,\\,y=\\frac{35}{6}{t}^{6}+2{t}^{5}+9.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=7t+2,$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,2}=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=7t+2,$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{5}+1)=5{t}^{4}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(7t+2)}{5{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(7)}{5{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{5{t}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,2}=\\frac{7}{5({2}^{4})}=\\frac{7}{80}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16dac5bd-acbd-51c6-8636-172e3a55aaf1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3697ea5a-686e-516d-b7fd-7445fbc0660a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266538} {:answer/id #uuid "b5739e7c-53f2-5c7f-9652-c0a0c6b6259c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266539} {:answer/id #uuid "a18de0a6-94fe-57d2-a4d0-ab6bbe39c322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266540} {:answer/id #uuid "319a4c57-e962-59a0-91f3-8d5b92eaaf01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266541} {:answer/id #uuid "0da525df-b37b-5e53-ac19-e0730966ddfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "319a4c57-e962-59a0-91f3-8d5b92eaaf01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266541} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ccbf2d7-ea9b-53a8-b333-f600349584c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266537}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d91b9b6d-416f-56f9-8f80-d7518f3295a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-78947" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x={t}^{3}-1,\\,y=3{t}^{6}+2$ where $t$ is a real number. Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=18{t}^{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{18{t}^{5}}{3{t}^{2}}=6{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(6{t}^{3})}{3{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18{t}^{2}}{3{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
